  "query_preview": "..\n\nDeep H3 to H5(sub 560dam) troughing is in place across all of the \nGreat Lakes this morning with surface extension of cold front now \npushing southward through portions of central IN/OH. Much cooler \nconditions in this setup as 850mb temperatures currently range \nbetween +4C to +6C areawide. Surface dewpoints have easily dropped \ninto the 40s for many areas and temperatures at daybreak are \nexpected to be in the lower 50s. Projected absolute vorticity charts \nsupport another vorticity maximum, collocated with composite trough \naxis, digging into the Lake Huron between 18-00z today.  Northerly \ngradient with legitimate cold pool aloft over Ontario results in \nfresh isentropic downglide and cold advection over the area today. \nGusty well  mixed northwest surface winds of 10 to 25 mph today.\nForecast soundings support high static stability in the 6.0 to 16.0 \nkft agl yielding dry conditions but with some boundary layer cumulus \nthis afternoon. \n\nFlow trajectories rapidly evolve to anticylonic tonight as strong \nsurface high pressure builds into Wisconsin. Main narrative tonight \nis very chill nightitme temperatures with the potential for some \nareas outside of the Detroit urban heat island to dip into the 40s.\nModerating and warming airmass between 4.0 and 8.0 kft agl will dry \nout any lingering low-mid relative humidity for the daytime \nThursday. Very comfortable weather will exist Thu with temperatures \nin the upper 70s and dewpoints in the lower 50s. Progressively \nwarmer Friday and Sunday with incremental increases in surface \nhumidity.  Latest indications are surface ridge axis will continue \nto hold through Southeast Michigan.\n\nJet exit region with anticyclonically wave break pushes into Lower \nMichigan for Sunday. Models suggest shortwave energy cascading in \nvicinity of the Southeast Michigan with return of warm advection. \nPersistent northwest flow could lead to a more active period early \nnext week with regards to precipitation. Current forecasts suggest \nhighs in the 80s early next week.",
